      "claim": "Mithramycin, a large glycosylated polyketide, showed a peak CSF:plasma ratio of only 0.12 in monkeys and little material in normal brains, indicating poor CNS penetration after systemic administration.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C11"],
      "claim": "GAZT, a glucuronide-conjugated nucleoside, poorly crossed the BBB in rhesus monkeys with a mean CSF/plasma ratio of 0.04, supporting poor CNS access for large glycosylated molecules.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "moderate",
  "decision_summary": "The query is a large polyene macrolide glycoside (amphotericin B-like). The most transferable evidence comes from mithramycin, a large glycosylated polyketide with poor CSF/plasma penetration, and GAZT, a glucuronide conjugate with very low CSF/plasma ratio. These support inadequate CNS access after systemic administration. Other records are aminoglycosides, small glycosides, or nucleotides with low structural transferability, and no credible positive evidence outweighs the negative signal.",
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct experimental record for amphotericin B itself is present.",
    "The most relevant analogue, mithramycin, is not identical in charge or polyene structure.",
    "No unbound brain or CSF exposure data for the exact query molecule are available."
  ],
